---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHero from "../components/PageHero.astro";
import ClosingBand from "../components/ClosingBand.astro";
import FramedPhoto from "../components/FramedPhoto.astro";
import photos from "../data/photos.json";
import imgRepair from "../assets/photos/tech-repair.jpg";

const machineSpecs = [
  { part: "Compute", detail: "Single-board computer, no accelerator, no cloud call" },
  { part: "Vision", detail: "Camera module in a fixed, controlled-light classification chamber" },
  { part: "Weight", detail: "Load cell for weight verification" },
  { part: "Insertion", detail: "Infrared sensor for insertion detection" },
  { part: "Gate", detail: "Servo-actuated — accepts or rejects" },
  { part: "Cabinet", detail: "Steel, fabricated locally" },
];

const repairPoints = [
  "Locally fabricated cabinet",
  "Commodity compute",
  "Standard connectors",
  "Parts obtainable in Lagos or Ibadan",
];
---

<Base
  title="Technology — Green Africa"
  description="Onboard computer vision, offline-first records and Algorand verification, built for African operating conditions."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Technology"
    headline="Engineered around the constraints, not despite them."
    subhead="Intermittent power. Patchy connectivity. Import costs that make European hardware a non-starter. Every technical decision here starts from those facts."
  />

  <section class="rule-t">
    <div class="container-x grid gap-10 py-16 sm:py-20 lg:grid-cols-12">
      <div class="lg:col-span-5" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow text-leaf">01 — The machine</p>
        <h2 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance">
          Choosing the constraint is the design.
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-5 text-base leading-relaxed text-ink/75 lg:col-span-6 lg:col-start-7" data-reveal style="--d:120ms">
        <p>
          The classification chamber is where the cleverness sits. By fixing the camera,
          controlling the lighting and taking one bottle at a time, we reduce a hard computer
          vision problem — object detection in an uncontrolled scene — to an easy one:
          single-object classification under known conditions. That's what lets a small quantized
          model run accurately on inexpensive hardware with no accelerator and no cloud call.
        </p>
        <p>
          Machines that accept a whole bag of mixed material need far more compute, far more
          mechanism and far more maintenance, to produce a less trustworthy count.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-2 border border-ink/15">
          <p class="border-b border-ink/15 bg-sand/60 px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-ink/60">
            Inside a station
          </p>
          <dl>
            {
              machineSpecs.map((spec) => (
                <div class="grid gap-1 border-b border-ink/10 px-4 py-3 last:border-b-0 sm:grid-cols-3 sm:gap-4">
                  <dt class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-ink/50">
                    {spec.part}
                  </dt>
                  <dd class="text-sm text-ink/75 sm:col-span-2">{spec.detail}</dd>
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </dl>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="rule-t">
    <div class="container-x grid gap-10 py-16 sm:py-20 lg:grid-cols-12">
      <div class="lg:col-span-5" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow text-leaf">02 — Offline-first by default</p>
        <h2 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance">
          Designed to work with no network at all.
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-5 text-base leading-relaxed text-ink/75 lg:col-span-6 lg:col-start-7" data-reveal style="--d:120ms">
        <p>
          Records are signed on the device the moment a deposit is verified, then written to a
          local hash-chained store. Each record includes the hash of the one before it, so any
          later alteration breaks the chain visibly. When connectivity returns, the queue submits
          to the ledger in order.
        </p>
        <p>
          Practically: the machine keeps paying, keeps counting and keeps its evidential integrity
          through an outage. Nothing is lost and nothing can be quietly rewritten in the gap.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="rule-t">
    <div class="container-x grid gap-10 py-16 sm:py-20 lg:grid-cols-12">
      <div class="lg:col-span-5" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow text-leaf">03 — Why a public ledger</p>
        <h2 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance">
          A filing cabinet nobody can break into.
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-5 text-base leading-relaxed text-ink/75 lg:col-span-6 lg:col-start-7" data-reveal style="--d:120ms">
        <p>
          We record verified recycling activity on Algorand so the recovery record can be checked
          independently by sponsors, auditors and partners who do not need to trust our database.
        </p>
        <p>
          A database we control is only ever as credible as our reputation. A public ledger gives
          each accepted session an independently verifiable transaction history while GreenAfrica
          keeps private user information off-chain.
        </p>
        <p>
          The application records RVM status, PET totals, Green Points, unique session IDs and
          hashed receipts. Duplicate session IDs are rejected, and application events can be read
          through Algorand indexing infrastructure for reporting and audit workflows.
        </p>
        <p>
          Recyclers do not need to manage crypto wallets. GreenAfrica's server-managed operator
          submits the application calls after the machine and backend have verified the action.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="bg-pine text-paper">
    <div class="container-x grid gap-10 py-16 sm:py-20 lg:grid-cols-12">
      <div class="lg:col-span-5" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow text-leaf-bright">04 — Built to be fixed</p>
        <h2 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance">
          Still running in year three.
        </h2>
        <FramedPhoto
          src={imgRepair}
          alt={photos["tech-repair"].alt}
          credit={`Photo: ${photos["tech-repair"].photographer} / Pexels`}
          dark
          class="mt-8"
        />
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-6 text-base leading-relaxed text-paper/75 lg:col-span-6 lg:col-start-7" data-reveal style="--d:120ms">
        <ul class="flex flex-wrap gap-2.5">
          {
            repairPoints.map((point) => (
              <li class="border border-mint/25 px-3.5 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-mint">
                {point}
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
        <p>
          A machine that can only be repaired by flying in a technician isn't infrastructure — it's
          a demonstration. We're building the version that's still running in year three.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <ClosingBand
    headline="Technical due diligence welcome."
    body="If you want to look at the record structure, query the ledger yourself, or pull apart the verification logic, we'll walk you through it."
  />
</Base>
